"""`Dashboard`: a live dashboard of a task and its builds in place of the
console and Bazel's terminal UI, on the terminal's alternate screen, for
`build`, `test`, `lint`, `gazelle` and `format` (`_KINDS`). Off by default:

    aspect build //... --dashboard

It takes the screen when the task starts and shows:

- a status bar with the task, how long it has run, and Bazel's status
  while a build runs, once the task has ended only what its ✔/✗ cannot
  say, else the task's phase and its latest update;
- each build that ended folded to a row (its verdict, command, actions,
  failures and time, the newest last, `+N earlier` past three), and while a
  build runs, or `p` asks, its panes:
  - a bar of the build's actions (`action_split`, one unit throughout):
    ran ok `█`, disk and remote cache hits `▓`, the local action cache
    `▒`, internal `░`, other `▌`, failed `╳`, not run `○`, done but not
    reported `┈`, then not done `─`, each kind its own glyph so they stay
    apart without colour; while it runs from the actions Bazel reports
    with every one published (`--dashboard:actions=all`, the default:
    internal ones have no command line), the execution log's cache hits
    and the done count's gap (the local action cache, estimated, after a
    failure too), and with only failures published (`failed`, or a build
    whose events Bazel sends out itself:
    `publish_all_actions_unless_sent_out`), `unreported` for what is
    neither ran nor a hit; `other` only at the end, what of Bazel's own
    counts fits no kind; at its right end how far it is (`3,830 of 8,563+ actions`,
    `+` while Bazel analyses), at the end its actions and cache hits
    (`8,590 actions · 3,148 cache hits (37%)`); a build `<`/`>` picked,
    its own;
  - the actions running now, from Bazel's own progress area, oldest first,
    yellow past a minute and red past five; once the build has finished,
    Bazel's critical path and the slowest actions seen running;
  - links to the invocation's results pages: the Aspect Build Results page
    the CLI streams to, and the BES results page Bazel streams to itself
    with `--bes_results_url`, each when there is one (OSC 8 hyperlinks);
  - a Results pane: targets, tests with those finished a cache gave whole
    (`7 passed (5 cached)`) and, while a `test` build runs, the test
    targets running and pending (`test_progress`), each kind of action
    there is any of in words, the bar's glyph a coloured key before its
    count, the cache hits as one group with their total (`action_results`;
    while the build runs, ran, internal and failed from the events, disk
    and remote settling as Bazel writes its execution log; at its end the
    split from its `BuildMetrics`), the critical path, and the failures
    counted (a click shows them);
- the main pane's tabs, always in one order, the shown one named by what is
  open in it (`Failures ✗1 ▹ //pkg:t ▹ attempt 1/2 ▹ a.py:12`):
  - Console: the task's whole output, from its start (what the CLI wrote
    before Bazel's console began, then Bazel's, then the CLI's own lines,
    as written), scrollable, in the colours it was written in (the theme's,
    Bazel's recoloured by it), the
    lines under each `INFO: From` folded to a count;
  - Failures: a row for each of the failure summary's entries, as it heads
    them (`failure_entries`: every failed action and test, every root cause
    Bazel's events report; a test's other attempts, shards and runs a `}`
    `{` away), the chosen one's excerpt under it (`record_excerpt`; every
    one's with Ctrl+O), then the targets a failed dependency blocked, then,
    once the build has ended, Bazel's other errors (`console_ties`);
    Enter opens one's log in the tab (its stderr and
    stdout, a test's `test.log`, found where Bazel left it, its URI's path
    decoded and a non-ASCII name either way Bazel spells it on disk, a remote
    cache's `bytestream://` one in the output tree); `a` lists the actions that
    succeeded and wrote output instead (`--dashboard:actions=all`, the
    default);
  - Metrics: the actions, each kind with what makes it up (the runners'
    spawns, remote and disk cache hits apart, the internal kinds), Bazel's
    action cache with why it missed, the execution log's spawns; tests,
    targets, time and memory, the slowest actions;
  - Invocation: the command, ids, Bazel's version, the flags given,
    workspace status and build metadata, what may be a secret redacted;
  - Tips: the task's tips the screen shows (`SURFACE_DASHBOARD`), the
    most urgent first, counted in the tab in the most urgent one's colour;
    the chosen one's body under it, and `s` silences it on this machine
    (`~/.aspect/config.json`, `silence_tip`) or undoes that, the row left
    muted saying so; the scrollback names what was silenced and how to undo
    it;
  - before any build, Failures, Metrics and Invocation muted, saying `No
    build yet`; with several builds, Metrics and Invocation show the newest,
    `<`/`>` another (`Metrics ▹ 1 of 2`), and Failures the newest's;
- a row of the keys for what has focus, and `?` for those that apply now,
  grouped (both from `KEYS`, which `on_key` dispatches through), then what
  the actions' glyphs are.

The keys, as in `less`, lazygit and Claude Code's transcript: `↑`/`↓` or
`k`/`j` scroll a line and the wheel three; Shift or Option with `↑`/`↓`,
`u`/`d` and Ctrl+`u`/`d` half a page; Page Up/Down, `b` and Space a page;
Shift+`K` or Home the top, Shift+`J` or End back to following (a list's
first and last row), as in Claude Code's transcript. Tab and
Shift+Tab cycle the tabs (or a click); `]`/`[` step the Failures list's
rows, showing each one's log (or, for a blocked target, an exit code or one
of Bazel's other errors, what it says) in Failures, `}`/`{` the next or
previous attempt, shard or run of the same target; Esc goes back a crumb, then to the tab's own pane,
then to the console. A click on a path shows the file in place (a peek,
highlighted by `std.syntax`), `o` opens what is shown in the editor
`editor` in `~/.aspect/config.json` or `--console:file-hyperlink-format`
names, and `O` picks another; a click on a web link opens it. The screen
takes the mouse: a drag selects and copies, a double click a word; `m`
(or `--dashboard:mouse=false`) gives it back to the terminal. `e` cycles
every line, errors and warnings, errors only; `w` cuts long lines rather
than wrap them; Ctrl+O unfolds every fold and folds them all again
(forgetting what clicks opened), a click on one (its `INFO: From` or its
count) just that one; `z` gives the main pane the whole screen;
`q` cancels the build, as Ctrl+C does.

Ctrl+F or `/` opens a search bar for a regular expression (`std.re`),
smart case unless Ctrl+T (or Alt+C) makes it match case, whole words with
Ctrl+B (or Alt+W). As you type it jumps to the newest match at or above the
view; Tab in the bar filters to the matching lines instead. Enter keeps it,
and `n`/`N` then step to the next and previous match, round to the other
end past the last; Esc clears it and
goes back to where the bar opened.

When the task ends the screen stays up, its tabs still browsable, until
`q`, Enter or Esc with nothing open, or Ctrl+C, whatever the outcome
(`--dashboard:stay=always`, the default); with `failure` it stays when
anything failed and otherwise counts down ten seconds and leaves unless a
key stops it; with `never` it leaves at once. `build` and `test` keep their
build's panes open on it, the others their builds folded; a task that
failed before any build leaves at once, its error last in the scrollback.
Then the scrollback gets the console as the screen showed it
(`replay_lines`), the flaky tests, the results pages, and the failure
summary the plain console prints too (`failure_summary`: `── Failures
(N) ──`, each failure Bazel's build events report once, what Bazel's
console ties to it exactly, its log's path (the history's copy, which
outlives Bazel's) and the targets it blocked, then Bazel's other errors,
the counts, Bazel's raw log and `aspect history show <id>` for the rest;
`report_lines`). The summary is written only there, never into the
screen's Console tab nor the task's output it dumps, so a run replayed
from the history shows it no more than the screen did.

How it is built:

1. `BazelTrait.output_display = "none"`: Bazel's console is still captured
   and run through every stage, but nothing of it is shown.
2. A `pre_task` hook takes the terminal (`ctx.std.term.take()`, the mouse
   taken with `screen.mouse(True)` unless asked not to, the wheel sent as
   arrows with `screen.wheel_keys(True)` while it is the terminal's), what
   the task wrote before it first in the console (`screen.earlier()`).
   Until a build draws, an idle hook (`ctx.hooks.idle`), which the runtime
   calls about every 100ms while the task waits on a child or sleeps,
   reads the CLI's lines and the keys and redraws.
3. A `BazelTrait.output_frame` hook, which the runtime calls about every
   100ms while a build's console is open, reads what the console would
   have shown (`build.output_feed()`, through `add_lines`), reads the keys
   (`screen.key()`, through `on_key`), and redraws the whole screen in one
   write (`render`) when it changed (Ctrl+L redraws it anyway); its first
   frame starts the build's own counts (`new_build`), a retry's too. The
   finished screen, idle, reads a key a second.
4. `build_event` hooks (`collect_event`, the plain console's too) count
   targets and tests from the build event stream, gather the failures and
   their root causes (`note_event`, `note_blocked`) and the actions that
   wrote output with their logs (`output_of`), and what finding a test's
   log in the output tree takes (the exec root, each configuration's output
   folder, each test's run and shard counts and last attempts).
5. An `output_end` hook folds the build to a row (`fold_build`), keeping
   what its tabs show for `<`/`>`, and draws.
6. A `post_task` hook keeps the screen up (or, with `stay=failure`, counts
   down, the task's exit code 0 and nothing failed), with Ctrl+C read as a
   key
   (`screen.interrupts(False)`) since the task has already ended, then gives
   the terminal back (`screen.release(replay_stderr = False, dump_lines = …)`,
   the task's output dumped to the scrollback as part of it, or, with no
   history to dump from, the console as it showed it after,
   `replay_lines`), then the summary.

A prompt reading stdin on the terminal, and a child marked
`interactive(True)` (`ctx.std.process.command`), get the terminal back for
as long as they run: the task's output so far goes to the scrollback, and
the screen comes back, redrawn, after. An unmarked child gets no terminal
while the screen is up (stdin from `/dev/null`, no controlling terminal), so
mark a spawn interactive if it may prompt.

On a terminal only, and never on CI; any other task keeps its console,
saying so in a line on stderr. Under a coding agent, with `TERM` dumb or
unset, or on a terminal under 40x12, it stays off even when enabled, saying
why in a line on stderr (`refusal`): an agent's pty has nobody to press the
keys the finished screen waits for.
"""

load("@aspect//bazel.axl", "BazelTrait", "ExecLogHook")
load("@aspect//feature/dashboard/actions.axl", "dashboard_actions")
load("@aspect//feature/dashboard/keys.axl", "dashboard_keys")
load("@aspect//feature/dashboard/log.axl", "dashboard_log")
load("@aspect//feature/dashboard/pointer.axl", "dashboard_pointer")
load("@aspect//feature/dashboard/render.axl", "dashboard_render")
load("@aspect//feature/dashboard/view.axl", "dashboard_view")
load("@aspect//traits.axl", "Phases")
load("@std//time.axl", "monotonic")
load("../private/lib/agent.axl", "under_agent")
load("../private/lib/aspect_flags.axl", "publish_all_actions_unless_sent_out")
load("../private/lib/bazel_results.axl", "bb_clientd_root", "bes_get")
load("../private/lib/failure_records.axl", "failure_records")
load("../private/lib/failures.axl", "failures")
load("../private/lib/results_pages.axl", "bes_results_url", "results_pages")
load("../private/lib/term_text.axl", "term_text")
load("../private/lib/tips.axl", "SURFACE_DASHBOARD", "collect_tips_sorted", "silence_tip", "unsilence_tip")

_c = term_text.c
_plain = term_text.plain
_RESET = term_text.RESET
ANSI = term_text.ANSI
CONTROLS = term_text.CONTROLS
_flaky = failure_records.flaky
build_outcome = failure_records.build_outcome
build_succeeded = failure_records.build_succeeded
collect_event = failure_records.collect_event
count_spawn = failure_records.count_spawn
note_log_file = failure_records.note_log_file
_failure_counts = failures.failure_counts
action_split = dashboard_actions.action_split
actions_text = dashboard_actions.actions_text
built = dashboard_actions.built
note_progress = dashboard_actions.note_progress
note_spawn_time = dashboard_actions.note_spawn_time
_MAX_LOG = dashboard_log.MAX_LOG
add_lines = dashboard_log.add_lines
new_search = dashboard_log.new_search
replay_lines = dashboard_log.replay_lines
report_lines = dashboard_log.report_lines
refresh_test_view = dashboard_view.refresh_test_view
mouse_key = dashboard_pointer.mouse_key
_LEAVE_KEYS = dashboard_keys.LEAVE_KEYS
_MAX_KEYS = dashboard_keys.MAX_KEYS
on_key = dashboard_keys.on_key
console_height = dashboard_render.console_height
render = dashboard_render.render

# The tasks whose screen stays up after their build: the others go on to
# run something that needs the terminal.
# The tasks the screen is for, taken from their start; any other keeps the
# console.
_KINDS = ["build", "test", "lint", "gazelle", "format"]

# How long a finished screen with nothing failed stays up (`stay =
# "failure"`), counting down.
_COUNTDOWN_MS = 10000

def attempt_state(now_ms: int) -> dict:
    """What a Bazel attempt starts with: its own counts, console, failures
    and actions, nothing open, timed from `now_ms`."""
    return dict(
        failure_records.new_state(),
        fresh = False,
        phase = "Starting Bazel…",
        selected = 0,
        picked = 0,
        cache = None,
        unseen = 0,
        focus = "console",
        view = None,
        log = [],
        log_size = 0,
        views = [],
        parked = {},
        seeded = 0,
        seed_trimmed = 0,
        tab_scroll = 0,
        hover = None,
        select = None,
        kinds = [],
        block = "",
        closing = [],
        closing_tail = [],
        closing_cut = 0,
        early = [],
        dropped = 0,
        unseen_errors = 0,
        slowest = {},
        critical = "",
        spawn_slowest = [],
        folded = [],
        fold_src = [],
        fold_head = [],
        fold_open = {},
        log_base = 0,
        fold_n = -1,
        fold_seen = 0,
        scroll = 0,
        start_ms = now_ms,
        bazel_uuid = "",
        exec_log_read = False,
        done_total = None,
        settled = 0,
    )

def _clear_build(state: dict):
    """What a build keeps beside `attempt_state`'s, which the next build and a
    retried attempt alike start without: the root causes it noted, and what
    was worked out from them."""
    state["root_causes"] = {}
    for key in ("blocked_cache", "entries_cache", "own_cache", "ties_cache"):
        state[key] = None

# What a build has of its own, which the next build (or a retried attempt)
# starts afresh; the console and what is open in it are the task's.
_PER_BUILD = ["fails", "outputs", "selected", "picked", "cache", "last_attempts", "spread", "test_counts", "counts", "inv", "closing", "closing_tail", "closing_cut", "slowest", "spawn_slowest", "critical", "bazel_uuid", "exec_log_read", "done_total", "bep_done_total", "settled", "excerpts"]

def new_build(state: dict, now_ms: int):
    """Start the next build (or a retried attempt) in the task's screen: its
    own counts, failures and invocation, timed from `now_ms`. The console is
    the task's, so it, the search over it (its current match, where the bar
    opened, whether it is anchored there) and what is open in it are kept.
    The Failures list, if focused, goes back to the console, as its rows are
    the old build's."""
    fresh = attempt_state(now_ms)
    for key in _PER_BUILD:
        state[key] = fresh[key]
    _clear_build(state)
    state["progress"] = []
    state["fresh"] = False
    state["build_failed"] = False
    state["build_start_ms"] = now_ms
    state["picked_build"] = None
    if state.get("focus") == "failures":
        state["focus"] = "console"

def end_build(state: dict, now_ms: int):
    """A build's end, inside `wait()`: its execution log was read to its end
    before then, so the build folded to a row (`fold_build`; the task goes
    on, and its finished screen shows its builds) keeps that it was."""
    state["exec_log_read"] = True
    fold_build(state, now_ms)
    state["building"] = False

def fold_build(state: dict, now_ms: int):
    """Keep the build that just ended as a folded row (`build_row`) and what
    its tabs show, for `<`/`>` to pick it again."""
    failed, blocked = _failure_counts(state)
    started = state.get("inv", {}).get("build_started")
    state.setdefault("builds", []).append(dict(
        command = str(bes_get(started, "command", "") or "") or state.get("build_command", "") or "bazel",
        actions = actions_text(action_split(dict(state, building = False))),
        failed = failed,
        blocked = blocked,
        ok = failed == 0 and not state["counts"]["tests_failed"] and not state.get("build_failed") and build_succeeded(state.get("inv") or {}),
        outcome = build_outcome(state.get("inv") or {}),
        ms = max(0, now_ms - state.get("build_start_ms", now_ms)),
        snapshot = dict({k: state.get(k) for k in _BUILD_KEYS}, building = False, blocked_cache = None, entries_cache = None, own_cache = None, ties_cache = None, excerpts = {}),
    ))

# What a folded build keeps for its tabs.
_BUILD_KEYS = ["counts", "inv", "slowest", "spawn_slowest", "critical", "pages", "fails", "outputs", "test_counts", "last_attempts", "spread", "root_causes", "bes_base", "bazel_uuid", "exec_log_read", "done_total", "bep_done_total", "log_path", "bazel_errors"]

# How long the finished screen waits for a key while nothing on it moves.
_IDLE_MS = 1000

# The smallest terminal the screen draws in.
_MIN_COLS = 40

_MIN_ROWS = 12

def refusal(std):
    """Why the screen does not take this terminal, for a line on stderr, or
    "" when it does; None when it is not one to ask (not a terminal, CI),
    where Bazel's console is what is wanted. Under a coding agent the
    screen would wait for keys nobody types, so it refuses even when
    enabled."""
    io = std.io
    if not (io.stdout.is_tty and io.stderr.is_tty) or std.env.var("CI"):
        return None
    if under_agent(std):
        return "dashboard off: a coding agent is running this; Bazel's console is shown instead"
    term = std.env.var("TERM") or ""
    if term in ("", "dumb"):
        return "dashboard off: TERM is %s; Bazel's console is shown instead" % ("dumb" if term else "not set")
    size = std.term.size()
    if size == None:
        return None
    if size[0] < _MIN_COLS or size[1] < _MIN_ROWS:
        return "dashboard off: the terminal is %dx%d, under %dx%d; Bazel's console is shown instead" % (size[0], size[1], _MIN_COLS, _MIN_ROWS)
    return ""

def new_state(**fields) -> dict:
    """A Dashboard's whole state before its task starts: a first attempt's
    (`attempt_state`), nothing taken or read yet, with `fields` set over
    it (the task's arguments and runtime, or a test's)."""
    state = attempt_state(0)
    state.update(
        screen = None,
        task = "",
        start_ms = 0,
        now_ms = 0,
        frames = 0,
        progress = [],
        filter = "all",
        search = new_search(),
        re = None,
        controls = None,
        list = "failures",
        help = False,
        zoom = False,
        mouse = False,
        line_links = {},
        frame = [],
        pane = (-1, -1),
        excerpt = None,
        cols = 80,
        osc = "",
        note = "",
        clock = None,
        process = None,
        countdown_ms = None,
        all_actions = True,
        ended = False,
        pages = [],
        bes_base = "",
        update = {},
    )
    state.update(fields)
    return state

def _dashboard_impl(ctx: FeatureContext):
    why = refusal(ctx.std)
    if why != "":
        if why:
            ctx.std.io.stderr.write(why + "\n")
        return

    state = new_state(
        mouse = ctx.args.mouse,
        excerpt = ctx.std.excerpt,
        clock = lambda: int(monotonic() * 1000),
        all_actions = ctx.args.actions == "all",
    )

    def _add_lines(lines, links = None, colours = None, cli = False):
        add_lines(state, lines, links, colours, cli)

    def _draw(screen, frame):
        """Write `frame` unless it is the one on the screen; whether it was
        written."""
        if frame == state.get("drawn"):
            return False
        screen.write(frame)
        state["drawn"] = frame
        return True

    def _add_output(screen):
        # The CLI's own lines, marked as such, their links moved with them.
        got = screen.output(with_links = True, with_colour = True)
        _add_lines([line for line, _, _ in got], [links for _, links, _ in got], [coloured for _, _, coloured in got], cli = True)

    def _take(task_ctx):
        """Take the terminal for the task, from its start: what it writes
        is captured into the console from here on."""
        state["screen"] = task_ctx.std.term.take()
        state["drawn"] = None

        # What the task wrote before the screen (the banner, its header),
        # first in the console; on the main screen already, so not
        # replayed on leaving.
        early = state["screen"].earlier()
        state["seeded"] = state.get("seeded", 0) + len(early)
        _add_lines([line for line, _, _ in early], [links for _, links, _ in early], [coloured for _, _, coloured in early], cli = True)

        # The mouse taken unless asked not to (`m` outlives a screen the
        # runtime took back), and its wheel as arrows while it is the
        # terminal's.
        state["screen"].wheel_keys(True)
        if state["mouse"]:
            state["screen"].mouse(True)
        state["re"] = task_ctx.std.re
        state["cli_version"] = task_ctx.std.env.aspect_cli_version() or ""
        state["read"] = task_ctx.std.fs.try_read_tail
        state["ansi"] = task_ctx.std.re.compile(ANSI)
        state["controls"] = task_ctx.std.re.compile(CONTROLS)
        state["env"] = task_ctx.std.env
        state["bb_root"] = bb_clientd_root(task_ctx.std.env)
        state["process"] = task_ctx.std.process
        state["io"] = task_ctx.std.io
        state["fs"] = task_ctx.std.fs
        state["syntax"] = task_ctx.std.syntax
        state["cancellation"] = task_ctx.cancellation
        state["task"] = task_ctx.task.kind
        state["start_ms"] = int(monotonic() * 1000)
        state["task_start_ms"] = state["start_ms"]
        state["silencer"] = lambda id, on: silence_tip(task_ctx, id) if on else unsilence_tip(task_ctx, id)
        state["tips_from"] = lambda: collect_tips_sorted(task_ctx, surface = SURFACE_DASHBOARD)

    def _pre(task_ctx):
        if task_ctx.task.kind not in _KINDS:
            task_ctx.std.io.stderr.write("Dashboard: for %s; %s shows its console\n" % (", ".join(_KINDS), task_ctx.task.kind))
            return
        if task_ctx.std.term.taken:
            return
        _take(task_ctx)
        _tick(state["screen"])

    def _tips():
        # The task's tips as they stand, for the Tips tab.
        if state.get("tips_from") != None:
            state["tips"] = state["tips_from"]()

    def _tick(screen):
        """One frame while no build draws them: the CLI's new lines, the keys
        waiting, the screen redrawn if it changed. Never inside itself: a key
        that waits (a click's opener) runs the idle hook."""
        if state.get("ticking"):
            return
        state["ticking"] = True
        _tips()
        state["now_ms"] = int(monotonic() * 1000)
        state["frames"] += 1
        _add_output(screen)
        cols, rows = screen.size()
        page = console_height(state, cols, rows)
        for _ in range(_MAX_KEYS):
            key = screen.key()
            if key == None:
                break
            on_key(state, key, page)
        _draw(screen, render(state, cols, rows))
        state["ticking"] = False

    def _idle(task_ctx):
        # Between builds, and before the first: a build's frames draw while
        # it runs, and the finished screen draws itself.
        screen = state["screen"]
        if screen == None or not screen.held or state.get("building") or state["ended"]:
            return
        _tick(screen)

    def _frame(task_ctx, build):
        # A build whose console is shown (its own `output =`) is left alone.
        feed = build.output_feed()
        if feed == None:
            return

        # The runtime gives a screen back on a hook's error.
        if state["screen"] != None and not state["screen"].held:
            state["screen"] = None
        if state["screen"] == None:
            return

        # The next build, or a retried attempt of this one: its own counts
        # and failures, in the task's console.
        now = int(monotonic() * 1000)
        if not state.get("building") or state["fresh"]:
            new_build(state, now)
            state["building"] = True
        state["build"] = build
        state["ended"] = False
        state["pages"] = results_pages(state["bes_base"], state["bazel_uuid"], state["update"])
        screen = state["screen"]
        state["now_ms"] = now
        state["frames"] += 1
        _add_lines(feed["lines"], feed.get("links"), feed.get("colours"))
        state["dropped"] = state.get("dropped", 0) + (feed.get("dropped", 0) or 0)

        # A frame that caught Bazel between drawing its progress area and
        # redrawing it keeps the last one, which the header shows too.
        if feed["progress"]:
            state["progress"] = feed["progress"]
            state["phase"] = feed["progress"][0]
            note_progress(state, feed["progress"])
        _add_output(screen)
        if state["frames"] % 10 == 0:
            _tips()
        cols, rows = screen.size()
        page = console_height(state, cols, rows)

        # Every key waiting, then one frame: a burst of wheel or drag
        # reports costs one render, never a backlog.
        for _ in range(_MAX_KEYS):
            key = screen.key()
            if key == None:
                break
            on_key(state, key, page)
        _draw(screen, render(state, cols, rows))

    def _event(task_ctx, event):
        kind = event.kind
        if kind == "build_started":
            state["bazel_uuid"] = str(bes_get(event.payload, "uuid", "") or "")
        elif kind == "options_parsed":
            state["bes_base"] = bes_results_url(event) or state["bes_base"]
        collect_event(state, event)
        refresh_test_view(state, event)

    def _update(task_ctx, update):
        if update.data:
            state["update"] = update.data

    def _attempt_end(task_ctx, exit_code):
        # The execution log is read to its end before an attempt ends.
        state["bazel_exit"] = exit_code
        state["fresh"] = True
        state["exec_log_read"] = True

    def _end(task_ctx, build):
        # Bazel's raw console, and what of it the failure summary and the
        # Failures list tie Bazel's lines from (`console_ties`).
        state["log_path"] = build.output_log or ""
        state["bazel_errors"] = build.output_bazel_errors()
        screen = state["screen"]
        if screen == None or not screen.held:
            return
        state["cancelling"] = state["cancelling"] or task_ctx.cancellation.root.cancelled
        state["build_failed"] = bool(build.output_fatal)

        end_build(state, int(monotonic() * 1000))
        state["progress"] = []
        cols, rows = screen.size()
        _draw(screen, render(state, cols, rows))

    def _stay(task_ctx, screen, countdown_ms):
        """Keep the finished screen up, scrollable, until a leave key, or
        until the run is told to stop (a SIGTERM), which no key would end.
        With a `countdown_ms`, it leaves by itself once that has gone by,
        unless a key comes first, which only stops it. The screen is the state's meanwhile, for
        the keys that use it (`m`)."""
        state["screen"] = screen

        # The end's tips, collected before the screen is given back, as the
        # Tips feature prints them only after.
        _tips()
        _hold(task_ctx, screen, countdown_ms)
        state["screen"] = None

    def _hold(task_ctx, screen, countdown_ms):
        # Keys typed while it finished were typed at a screen that read none,
        # meaning to cancel: not to leave the finished one now.
        for _ in range(1 << 20):
            if screen.key() == None:
                break
        screen.interrupts(False)
        deadline = int(monotonic() * 1000) + countdown_ms if countdown_ms else None
        for _ in range(1 << 30):
            if task_ctx.cancellation.root.cancelled or not screen.held:
                return
            if deadline != None:
                state["countdown_ms"] = max(0, deadline - int(monotonic() * 1000))
                if state["countdown_ms"] == 0:
                    return
            _add_output(screen)
            cols, rows = screen.size()
            changed = _draw(screen, render(state, cols, rows))

            # Idle, a key a second; a frame that moves, or the countdown, ten.
            key = screen.key(timeout_ms = 100 if changed or deadline != None else _IDLE_MS)
            if key == "ctrl-c":
                return
            if key != None and deadline != None and key.startswith("mouse-move:"):
                # The pointer passing over the screen: lit, the countdown
                # left running.
                mouse_key(state, key)
            elif key != None and deadline != None:
                # A leave key leaves now; any other stops the countdown and
                # does nothing else.
                if key in _LEAVE_KEYS:
                    return
                deadline = None
                state["countdown_ms"] = None
            elif key != None:
                # This key and every one waiting behind it, then one frame.
                page = console_height(state, cols, rows)
                for _ in range(_MAX_KEYS):
                    if on_key(state, key, page):
                        return
                    key = screen.key()
                    if key == None or key == "ctrl-c":
                        break
                if key == "ctrl-c":
                    return

    def _post(task_ctx, outcome):
        screen = state["screen"]
        if screen == None:
            return
        state["screen"] = None
        if screen.held:
            # Not for a cancelled task (`q`, or Ctrl+C): the CLI is on its way
            # out, and would force the exit before a key came.
            if task_ctx.cancellation.root.cancelled:
                state["cancelling"] = True
            state["verdict"] = outcome.exit_code == 0

            # A task that failed before any build has nothing to browse:
            # gone at once, its error last in the scrollback.
            early = not built(state) and outcome.exit_code != 0
            if ctx.args.stay != "never" and task_ctx.std.io.stdin.is_tty and not state["cancelling"] and not early:
                state["ended"] = True
                state["progress"] = []
                state["phase"] = "Done"
                c = state["counts"]

                # A flaky test's failed attempt is no failure: it passed.
                passed = outcome.exit_code == 0 and not [r for r in state["fails"] if not _flaky(state, r)] and not c["failures"] and not c["tests_failed"]
                _stay(task_ctx, screen, _COUNTDOWN_MS if passed and ctx.args.stay == "failure" else 0)
            _add_output(screen)

            # The scrollback written as part of the release, so no line
            # written meanwhile shows twice or ahead of it (`_report`).
            rest = screen.release(replay_stderr = False, dump_lines = _MAX_LOG, older = _older(task_ctx))
            _add_lines([_plain(line) for line in rest], None, rest, cli = True)
        _report(task_ctx)

    def _older(task_ctx):
        """The dump's line for the lines it leaves out."""
        return "… {n} older lines: aspect history show " + task_ctx.task.id

    def _report(task_ctx):
        """What the scrollback keeps once the screen is gone: the task's
        output since the screen was taken, as `output.ansi` keeps it
        (`ctx.std.term.dump_output`), else the console as the screen showed
        it (`replay_lines`), then the failures, the counts and where the
        rest is (`report_lines`)."""
        err = task_ctx.std.io.stderr

        # The task's output as a run without the screen leaves it, from
        # output.ansi; the console as the screen showed it without one. Both
        # are in output.log already: not copied again.
        if not task_ctx.std.term.dump_output(max_lines = _MAX_LOG, older = _older(task_ctx)):
            for line in replay_lines(state, err.is_tty):
                err.write(line + "\n", copy = False)
        for line in report_lines(state, task_ctx.task.id, err.is_tty, replayed = True, linked = task_ctx.std.io.linked, excerpt = task_ctx.std.excerpt):
            err.write(line + "\n")
        gone = sorted(state.get("silenced_tips", {}).keys())
        if gone:
            err.write(_c("muted") + "Silenced on this machine: %s · undo: take it out of tips.silence in ~/.aspect/config.json" % ", ".join(gone) + _RESET + "\n")

    t = ctx.traits[BazelTrait]
    t.output_display = "none"
    if ctx.args.actions == "all":
        # Every action reported to this task alone, not to its BES sinks.
        publish_all_actions_unless_sent_out(t, state)

    # What the Failures tab and the summary tie Bazel's lines from, with the
    # Console's stages off too.
    t.output_stages.append(bazel.output.collect_errors())
    t.output_frame.append(_frame)
    t.output_end.append(_end)
    t.build_event.append(_event)

    def _spawn(task_ctx, entry):
        # The entry's kind is its `type`: a `spawn` or a `file`, as asked.
        what = bes_get(entry, "type")
        if type(what) == "file":
            note_log_file(state["counts"], bes_get(entry, "id", 0), what)
        else:
            count_spawn(state["counts"], what)
        note_spawn_time(state, bes_get(entry, "type"))

    t.exec_log_event.append(ExecLogHook(on_entry = _spawn, kinds = ["spawn", "file"], file_paths = ["/testlogs/"]))
    t.bazel_attempt_end.append(_attempt_end)
    ctx.traits[Phases].task_update.append(_update)
    ctx.hooks.pre_task(_pre)
    ctx.hooks.idle(_idle)
    ctx.hooks.post_task(_post)

Dashboard = feature(
    display_name = "Dashboard",
    summary = "Run build, test, lint, gazelle and format in a full-screen dashboard: the task's console, its builds' running actions and results, failures, metrics.",
    implementation = _dashboard_impl,
    enabled = False,
    args = {
        "stay": args.string(
            default = "always",
            values = ["always", "failure", "never"],
            bare = "always",
            description = "Whether the screen stays up once the task ends, until q, Enter, Esc or Ctrl+C: `always` (the default) stays whatever the outcome, to look at its tabs; `failure` stays when anything failed, and with nothing failed counts down 10 seconds and leaves, which q, Enter or Esc cut short and any other key stops; `never` leaves at once. Only when stdin is a terminal.",
        ),
        "mouse": args.boolean(
            default = True,
            description = "Have the screen take the mouse (the default): its wheel scrolls the console and never the terminal past the screen, a drag selects and copies, and a click opens a web link or a path in the editor --console:file-hyperlink-format names (any other link is copied). false leaves the mouse to the terminal, which then selects and opens links itself and sends the wheel as the arrow keys where it supports that. `m` switches between the two while the screen is up.",
        ),
        "actions": args.string(
            default = "all",
            values = ["failed", "all"],
            description = "Which actions Bazel reports to the screen: `all` (the default) adds --build_event_publish_all_actions for this task alone (BES backends the CLI streams to and a --build_event_binary_file get the extra events only if your own flags or rc ask; a --build_event_json_file or --build_event_text_file, which Bazel writes itself, gets them), so the Actions rows tell what ran, was a disk or remote cache hit or was internal while the build runs, and `a` lists the actions that wrote output; `failed` reports only those that failed (the screen then says `other` for the rest until the build's metrics come). A build with a --remote_cache or --remote_executor (unless --experimental_build_event_upload_strategy=local) or a --bes_backend of its own reports only failed actions either way: Bazel would send every action's event out itself, and with the cache down it stops the whole event stream at the first one.",
        ),
    },
)
